<?php
/**
 * @var \Laminas\View\Renderer\PhpRenderer $this
 * @var array $status
 * @var string $csrf
 */
$this->headLink()->appendStylesheet($this->assetUrl('css/history.css', 'RevisionHistory'));
$s = $this->revisionHistoryShow();
$e = [$s, 'e'];
$num = function ($n) { return $n === null ? '—' : number_format((int) $n); };
$last = $status['last'];
$tables = $last['tables'] ?? [];
$newTables = array_values(array_filter($tables, function ($t) { return !empty($t['new']); }));
$grew = array_values(array_filter($tables, function ($t) { return !empty($t['was']) && $t['rows'] !== null && $t['rows'] !== $t['was']; }));

echo $this->pageTitle('Checking the data itself', 1, 'Revision History');
?>
<div class="rh-wrap rh-copy">
<p class="rh-crumbs"><a href="<?= $e($this->url('admin/revision-history')) ?>">← Revision History</a></p>

<p>Everything saved through Omeka is heard as it happens. A module that writes the tables with its own SQL is not,
and neither is a change made straight in the database or a table put back from a backup.</p>

<p>So the data is looked at afterwards. One number is kept for what each item, media and item set holds — its own row, its values,
and the notes drawn on it. When that number is different from the last check and nothing in the history accounts for the difference,
an entry is written saying so, and it appears on the history page like any other.</p>

<?php $room = $status['room']; $mb = function ($b) { return $b >= 1048576 ? number_format($b / 1048576, 1) . ' MB' : number_format(max(0, $b) / 1024, 0) . ' KB'; }; ?>
<div class="rh-about">
<?php if ($status['copy']): ?>
    <p><strong>A copy of what each resource holds is being kept</strong> (<?= number_format($status['copied']) ?> written down, <?= $e($mb($room['copy_now'])) ?>),
    so a change nobody recorded can say which values were added, taken away or changed — not only that something was.
    For comparison, Omeka's own <code>value</code> table is <?= $e($mb($room['value_table'])) ?>.</p>
    <form method="post">
        <input type="hidden" name="revision_history_csrf" value="<?= $e($csrf) ?>">
        <input type="hidden" name="revision_history_copy" value="off">
        <button type="submit">Stop keeping the copy and throw it away</button>
    </form>
<?php else: ?>
    <p><strong>Without a copy, a check says that something changed, not what</strong>: the number is a fingerprint, not a second copy of the data.
    Keeping a copy as well would take about <?= $e($mb($room['estimate'])) ?> here
    (<?= number_format($room['values']) ?> values, <?= number_format($room['value_length']) ?> characters each on average;
    Omeka's own <code>value</code> table is <?= $e($mb($room['value_table'])) ?>).
    It is written down on each check, only for what changed, so it costs room rather than time — once it is on, it keeps itself up to date with no further asking.</p>
    <form method="post">
        <input type="hidden" name="revision_history_csrf" value="<?= $e($csrf) ?>">
        <input type="hidden" name="revision_history_copy" value="on">
        <button type="submit">Keep a copy, so a change can say what it was</button>
    </form>
<?php endif; ?>
</div>

<div class="rh-about">
<?php $every = (int) $status['every']; ?>
    <p><?php if ($every): ?><strong>A check happens by itself</strong> when the last one is more than <?= $every ?> hour<?= $every === 1 ? '' : 's' ?> old:
    the next time somebody opens any page in the admin, one is started in the background. Nobody has to press anything.
    <?php else: ?><strong>Checks only happen when the button below is pressed.</strong><?php endif; ?>
    The question is asked on every admin page — anywhere in Omeka, not only here — so a site somebody is working in checks itself while they work.
    Asking costs one small query; the check itself runs in the background and no page waits for it. Nights and weekends are caught the next time somebody signs in.</p>
<?php
    // The same php that is running this page, when that is a php to run a file with.
    $php = defined('PHP_BINARY') && PHP_BINARY && strpos(basename(PHP_BINARY), 'php') === 0 && strpos(basename(PHP_BINARY), 'fpm') === false ? PHP_BINARY : 'php';
    $line = '0 * * * * ' . $php . ' ' . dirname(__DIR__, 4) . '/watch.php >/dev/null 2>&1';
?>
    <p>A server with cron can keep its own clock instead, which also works at night and at the weekend when nobody opens a page.
    Every hour, on the server itself:</p>
    <pre class="rh-cron"><?= $e($line) ?></pre>
    <p>Run that way, a check leaves no background job behind — it does the work itself. The two can be used together: cron for the quiet hours,
    and the setting below as a net for when cron is not there.</p>
    <form method="post" class="rh-filters">
        <input type="hidden" name="revision_history_csrf" value="<?= $e($csrf) ?>">
        <label><span>Check by itself</span>
            <select name="revision_history_every">
                <?php foreach ([1 => 'When the last one is an hour old', 4 => 'When the last one is four hours old', 24 => 'When the last one is a day old', 168 => 'When the last one is a week old', 0 => 'Never — only when the button is pressed'] as $hours => $word): ?>
                <option value="<?= (int) $hours ?>"<?= $every === (int) $hours ? ' selected' : '' ?>><?= $e($word) ?></option>
                <?php endforeach; ?>
            </select>
        </label>
        <div class="rh-go"><button type="submit">Save</button></div>
    </form>
</div>

<?php if ($status['running']): ?>
<div class="rh-warn"><strong>A check is running.</strong> Reload this page to see what it found.</div>
<?php endif; ?>

<table>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Items, media and item sets</td><td class="n"><?= $num($status['resources']) ?></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Of those, with a number written down</td><td class="n"><?= $num($status['remembered']) ?></td></tr>
        <tr><td>First check</td><td class="n"><?= $status['baseline'] ? $e($s->local((string) $status['baseline'])->format('j F Y, H:i')) : 'not yet' ?></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Last check</td><td class="n"><?= $last ? $e($s->local((string) $last['at'])->format('j F Y, H:i')) : '—' ?></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<?php if ($last && !empty($last['first'])): ?>
<p>The last check was the first one: <?= $num($last['checked']) ?> resources were written down as they were then.
Nothing could be reported, because there was nothing to compare against. From the next check on, a difference can be seen.</p>
<?php elseif ($last): $f = $last['found']; $ex = $last['examples'] ?? []; ?>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>What the last check found, that this history does not account for</th><th></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Changed</td><td class="n"><?= $num($f['changed'] ?? 0) ?></td></tr>
        <tr><td>No longer here</td><td class="n"><?= $num($f['gone'] ?? 0) ?></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Here, with nothing saying it was created</td><td class="n"><?= $num($f['appeared'] ?? 0) ?></td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<?php if (array_sum([(int) ($f['changed'] ?? 0), (int) ($f['gone'] ?? 0), (int) ($f['appeared'] ?? 0)])): ?>
<p>Each one has its own entry on the history page: <a href="<?= $e($this->url('admin/revision-history', [], ['query' => ['what' => 'edited']])) ?>">open the history</a>
and look for <em>found by checking the data itself</em>. The first few:
<?php foreach (['changed' => 'changed', 'gone' => 'gone', 'appeared' => 'new'] as $k => $word):
    if (empty($ex[$k])) continue; ?>
<br><?= $e($word) ?>:
<?php foreach ($ex[$k] as $id): ?><a href="<?= $e($this->url('admin/revision-history', ['action' => 'item', 'id' => (int) $id])) ?>">#<?= (int) $id ?></a> <?php endforeach;
endforeach; ?>
</p>
<?php else: ?>
<p>Nothing unaccounted for. Everything the data holds matches what the history says about it.</p>
<?php endif; ?>
<?php endif; ?>

<?php if (!$status['running']): ?>
<form method="post">
    <input type="hidden" name="revision_history_csrf" value="<?= $e($csrf) ?>">
    <button type="submit">Check now</button>
</form>
<p class="rh-about">Reads every resource once. It reads, and changes nothing, so it can be done often.
Nothing is changed by it: it only reads, writes down the numbers, and writes an entry for what nothing else accounts for.
This button is for checking straight away; otherwise it happens by itself, as set above.</p>
<?php endif; ?>

<?php if ($tables): ?>
<h3>Tables in this database</h3>
<p>A module that keeps its own record cannot be read here without somebody looking at that module first.
What can be seen without being told is a table appearing, growing or vanishing. That is what this list is for.</p>
<?php if ($newTables): ?>
<div class="rh-warn"><strong><?= count($newTables) ?> table<?= count($newTables) === 1 ? '' : 's' ?> seen for the first time:</strong>
<?php foreach ($newTables as $t): ?><code><?= $e($t['name']) ?></code> (<?= $num($t['rows']) ?>) <?php endforeach; ?>
</div>
<?php endif; ?>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Rows now</th><th>At the check before</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    <?php foreach ($tables as $t): ?>
    <tr>
        <td><code><?= $e($t['name']) ?></code><?= !empty($t['new']) ? ' <span class="rh-id">new</span>' : '' ?><?= !empty($t['gone']) ? ' <span class="rh-gone">gone</span>' : '' ?></td>
        <td class="n"><?= $t['rows'] === null ? '<span class="rh-gone">—</span>' : $num($t['rows']) ?></td>
        <td class="n"><?= $t['was'] === null ? '—' : $num($t['was']) ?></td>
    </tr>
    <?php endforeach; ?>
    </tbody>
</table>
<?php endif; ?>
</div>
